{
  "id": "lists",
  "title": "Lists",
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "01",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Create and Print a List",
      "sidebar_tag": "list",
      "description": [
        "<p>Create a list containing exactly these five numbers, in this order:",
        "<code>3, 1, 4, 1, 5</code>. Then print the list.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "[3, 1, 4, 1, 5]</pre>",
        "<p>A list is written with square brackets, with commas between the",
        "items &mdash; <code>[3, 1, 4, 1, 5]</code>. Printing a list shows all",
        "its items at once, brackets and all.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "# TODO: create a list containing 3, 1, 4, 1, 5 (in that order),",
        "# then print it"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [],
          "expected": "[3, 1, 4, 1, 5]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Reading Three Numbers into a List",
      "sidebar_tag": "list",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read three integers, one per line, into a single list, then print",
        "the list.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "20",
        "30",
        "[10, 20, 30]</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "# TODO: read three integers into a list, then print the list"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "2",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, 3]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "20",
            "30"
          ],
          "expected": "[10, 20, 30]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "-5",
            "0",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "[-5, 0, 5]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Access by Index",
      "sidebar_tag": "indexing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read three integers into a list, then read one more integer to use",
        "as an <strong>index</strong>, and print the item at that index.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "10",
        "20",
        "30",
        "2",
        "30</pre>",
        "<p>Negative indices work too &mdash; try an index of <code>-1</code> and",
        "see what you get.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "nums = [int(input()), int(input()), int(input())]",
        "idx = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: print nums at position idx"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "20",
            "30",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "10"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "20",
            "30",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "30"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10",
            "20",
            "30",
            "-1"
          ],
          "expected": "30"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "04",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Building a List of Unknown Size",
      "sidebar_tag": "for + append",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, then read <code>n</code> more integers",
        "into a list &mdash; one at a time, using a <code>for</code> loop and",
        "<code>.append()</code>, since you don't know in advance how many",
        "there'll be beyond that count. Print the list's length afterward.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "1",
        "2",
        "3",
        "3</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "nums = []",
        "",
        "# TODO: use a for loop over range(n) to read n integers and append",
        "# each one to nums. Then print len(nums)."
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "1",
            "2",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "3"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "1"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "5",
            "1",
            "2",
            "3",
            "4",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "05",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Append to a List",
      "sidebar_tag": "append()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Start with the list <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>, read one more integer,",
        "append it to the list, and print the result.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "4",
        "[1, 2, 3, 4]</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "nums = [1, 2, 3]",
        "",
        "# TODO: read an integer and append it to nums, then print nums"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "4"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, 3, 4]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "100"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, 3, 100]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "-1"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, 3, -1]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "06",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Loop Over a List",
      "sidebar_tag": "for",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, then read <code>n</code> integers into",
        "a list. Print each item in the list on its own line, in order.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "10",
        "20",
        "30",
        "10",
        "20",
        "30</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "nums = []",
        "",
        "# TODO: build the list from input as before, then loop over nums",
        "# and print each item on its own line"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "10",
            "20",
            "30"
          ],
          "expected": "10\n20\n30"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "7"
          ],
          "expected": "7"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "07",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Sum of a List",
      "sidebar_tag": "sum()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, then read <code>n</code> integers into",
        "a list, and print their sum using the built-in <code>sum()</code>",
        "function.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "1",
        "2",
        "3",
        "6</pre>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "nums = []",
        "",
        "# TODO: build the list from input as before, then print sum(nums)"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "1",
            "2",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "6"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "4",
            "10",
            "20",
            "30",
            "40"
          ],
          "expected": "100"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "5"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "0"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "08",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Find the Maximum",
      "sidebar_tag": "for",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read a count <code>n</code>, then read <code>n</code> integers into",
        "a list, and print the largest one &mdash; without using the built-in",
        "<code>max()</code> function.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "3",
        "7",
        "2",
        "7</pre>",
        "<p>A common pattern: start by assuming the first item is the biggest",
        "one you've seen, then loop through the rest, updating your answer",
        "whenever you find something bigger.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "nums = []",
        "",
        "# TODO: build the list from input, then find and print the",
        "# largest value in nums (without using max())"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "5",
            "3",
            "7",
            "2",
            "9",
            "4"
          ],
          "expected": "9"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "-1",
            "-5",
            "-2"
          ],
          "expected": "-1"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "42"
          ],
          "expected": "42"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "09",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "List Slicing",
      "sidebar_tag": "slicing",
      "description": [
        "<p>Start with the list <code>[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]</code>. Read a",
        "<code>start</code> and <code>end</code> index, and print the slice",
        "<code>nums[start:end]</code>.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "1",
        "3",
        "[20, 30]</pre>",
        "<p>Just like with strings, a slice includes the start index but stops",
        "before the end index.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]",
        "start = int(input())",
        "end = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: print nums[start:end]"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "[20, 30]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "[10, 20]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "2",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "[30, 40, 50]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Modifying a List Element",
      "sidebar_tag": "mutation",
      "description": [
        "<p>Start with the list <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>. Read an index and a new",
        "value, set the item at that index to the new value, and print the",
        "resulting list.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "0",
        "99",
        "[99, 2, 3]</pre>",
        "<p>Unlike strings, lists <strong>are</strong> mutable &mdash; a single",
        "item inside a list can be reassigned directly with",
        "<code>nums[i] = new_value</code>, no error, no need to build a whole",
        "new list.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "nums = [1, 2, 3]",
        "idx = int(input())",
        "val = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: set nums at position idx to val, then print nums"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "0",
            "99"
          ],
          "expected": "[99, 2, 3]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "2",
            "-5"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, -5]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "0"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 0, 3]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Removing an Item",
      "sidebar_tag": "remove()",
      "description": [
        "<p>Start with the list <code>[10, 20, 30, 40]</code>. Read a value,",
        "remove it from the list using <code>.remove()</code>, and print the",
        "resulting list.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "20",
        "[10, 30, 40]</pre>",
        "<p><code>.remove(value)</code> removes the first item that matches",
        "<code>value</code> &mdash; note that this is different from",
        "removing by <em>position</em>.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "nums = [10, 20, 30, 40]",
        "target = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: remove target from nums, then print nums"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "20"
          ],
          "expected": "[10, 30, 40]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "10"
          ],
          "expected": "[20, 30, 40]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "40"
          ],
          "expected": "[10, 20, 30]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "30"
          ],
          "expected": "[10, 20, 40]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "source_unit": "lists",
      "title": "Fix the Bug",
      "sidebar_tag": "debug",
      "description": [
        "<p>This code is supposed to print the <strong>last</strong> item in a",
        "list. Run it and see what actually happens.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "nums = [10, 20, 30]",
        "print(nums[len(nums)])"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [],
          "expected": "30"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
